---
import ContentPage from '../../layouts/ContentPage.astro';

const title = 'About LogTide and Giuseppe Pollio | LogTide';
const description = 'LogTide is open-source log management and SIEM, created and maintained by Giuseppe Pollio. Where it came from, who builds it and how the project is run.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'About', url: '/about/' },
];

const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: 'Giuseppe Pollio',
  url: 'https://logtide.dev/about/',
  description: 'Creator and maintainer of LogTide, and AWS Community Builder.',
  worksFor: { '@id': 'https://logtide.dev/#organization' },
  sameAs: ['https://dev.to/polliog', 'https://github.com/Polliog'],
};
---

<ContentPage
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  extraSchemas={[personSchema]}
  keywords={["about LogTide", "Giuseppe Pollio", "LogTide creator", "open source log management"]}
>
  <p class="font-mono text-xs text-primary-readable">about</p>
  <h1 class="mt-4">About LogTide</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    LogTide is open-source log management and SIEM for teams that want to own their data. You can run it on your
    own servers or use the managed EU cloud, and the core is the same in both.
  </p>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      LogTide started on November 26, 2025, with a first commit and a dev.to post a few days later:
      <em>I built an Open Source Datadog alternative because ELK was eating my RAM</em>. The goal has not changed
      since: logs, traces and threat detection in one product, without an Elasticsearch cluster to babysit.
    </p>
    <p>
      The numbers from the first year, good and bad, are in
      <a href="/blog/logtide-one-year/">Logtide: 1 Year After the First Commit</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="giuseppe-pollio">Who builds it</h2>
    <p>
      LogTide is created and maintained by Giuseppe Pollio, who also writes the articles on this blog. Giuseppe is an
      AWS Community Builder and publishes technical posts on
      <a href="https://dev.to/polliog" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dev.to</a>. Most of the code so far
      has been written by one person, with contributions from the community on
      <a href="https://github.com/logtide-dev/logtide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How the project is run</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The core is open source under the AGPLv3 license, and development happens in public.</li>
      <li>Release notes for every version are in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.</li>
      <li>Vulnerabilities can be reported privately to <a href="mailto:security@logtide.dev">security@logtide.dev</a>.</li>
      <li>Company and data processing details are in the <a href="/legal/terms/">terms</a> and the <a href="/legal/dpa/">data processing agreement</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the <a href="/docs/getting-started/">getting started guide</a>, see how LogTide compares in the
      <a href="/vs/">comparison pages</a>, or <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
